window.ESSAY_BANK = [

/* ---------- Chapter 1 ---------- */
{ id:"E1-1", ch:1, type:"essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:2,
  q:"Define investment banking and explain three ways in which it differs from commercial banking.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> An investment bank is a financial intermediary that helps corporations, governments and institutions raise capital and execute strategic transactions, and that trades and manages financial assets. It does not take retail deposits.</p><p>Three differences: (1) <b>Funding and product.</b> A commercial bank funds itself with deposits and earns the interest spread on loans; an investment bank earns fees, underwriting and trading spreads, and management fees. (2) <b>Client and risk.</b> Commercial banking is credit-risk driven with a diversified retail/SME base; investment banking is transaction- and market-risk driven with a concentrated institutional client base. (3) <b>Earnings profile.</b> Commercial banking income is comparatively stable and annuity-like; investment banking fee income is highly cyclical because it depends on deal and issuance volumes. A fourth acceptable point: regulation and deposit insurance apply to deposit-takers, while investment banks face capital-markets conduct and capital rules.</p>",
  rubric:"Definition correct incl. 'no retail deposits' (0.5) · three valid, clearly explained differences (1.2) · use of correct terminology (0.3)." },

{ id:"E1-2", ch:1, type:"essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:2,
  q:"Describe the front-, middle- and back-office structure of a modern investment bank, giving an example function of each, and explain why the 'Chinese wall' exists.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> The <b>front office</b> generates revenue and faces clients: M&A advisory, ECM/DCM, sales &amp; trading, research, asset management. The <b>middle office</b> measures and controls risk: market and credit risk management, compliance, treasury, product control. The <b>back office</b> processes and records: settlement, clearing, custody, finance, technology, HR.</p><p>The front office is further split into a <b>private side</b> (advisory bankers who hold material non-public information about clients) and a <b>public side</b> (sales, trading, research). The Chinese wall is the mandatory information barrier between them. Without it, knowledge of an unannounced acquisition could be used to trade ahead of the market — insider dealing — or to bias research. It is a legal and regulatory requirement, enforced by physical separation, restricted systems access, wall-crossing procedures and restricted lists.</p>",
  rubric:"Three tiers correctly identified with valid examples (1.0) · private/public side distinction (0.5) · correct legal rationale for the wall (0.5)." },

{ id:"E1-3", ch:1, type:"calc-essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:2,
  q:"A company is financed 65% equity and 35% debt. The risk-free rate is 3%, the equity risk premium 5.5%, its equity beta 1.2, its pre-tax cost of debt 7% and the corporate tax rate 20%. (a) Compute the cost of equity. (b) Compute the after-tax cost of debt. (c) Compute the WACC. (d) Explain what happens to WACC if the company issues more debt, and why this cannot continue indefinitely.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> (a) r<sub>e</sub> = r<sub>f</sub> + β(ERP) = 3% + 1.2 × 5.5% = <b>9.6%</b>. (b) r<sub>d</sub>(1−t) = 7% × (1 − 0.20) = <b>5.6%</b>. (c) WACC = 0.65 × 9.6% + 0.35 × 5.6% = 6.24% + 1.96% = <b>8.20%</b>.</p><p>(d) Substituting cheaper after-tax debt for equity initially <b>lowers</b> WACC, because interest is tax-deductible (the tax shield) and debt is senior and therefore cheaper. This cannot continue indefinitely: as leverage rises, equity becomes riskier so beta and r<sub>e</sub> increase; lenders demand a higher r<sub>d</sub>; and expected costs of financial distress (lost customers, forced asset sales, bankruptcy costs) grow. Beyond an optimal range these effects outweigh the tax shield and WACC turns upward — the trade-off theory of capital structure.</p>",
  rubric:"(a) 0.4 · (b) 0.4 · (c) 0.5 · (d) tax-shield benefit and rising distress/return costs (0.7)." },

/* ---------- Chapter 2 ---------- */
{ id:"E2-1", ch:2, type:"essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:3,
  q:"Walk through the main stages of an IPO from mandate to aftermarket stabilization, and explain at which point placement risk transfers from the issuer to the underwriter.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> Stages: (1) <b>Mandate / bake-off</b> — banks pitch valuation and strategy; the issuer appoints bookrunners. (2) <b>Due diligence and structuring</b> — business, financial and legal review; decide the primary/secondary split. (3) <b>Registration</b> — file the prospectus with the regulator. (4) <b>Valuation and price range</b> — comparable companies, precedent transactions and DCF produce an indicative range. (5) <b>Roadshow</b> — management markets to institutions. (6) <b>Bookbuilding</b> — underwriters collect price-and-size demand indications, building a demand curve. (7) <b>Pricing and allocation</b> — the final price is set, generally at a deliberate discount to expected trading value, and shares are allocated. (8) <b>Aftermarket stabilization</b> — the greenshoe supports the price for roughly 30 days.</p><p><b>Risk transfer:</b> in a firm-commitment deal, placement risk passes to the underwriter when the underwriting agreement is signed at pricing — the bank purchases the shares and is left holding any it cannot resell. Before that moment the risk sits with the issuer, and under a best-efforts structure it never transfers at all.</p>",
  rubric:"All main stages in correct sequence (1.5) · correct identification of risk transfer at pricing/signing (1.0) · firm-commitment vs best-efforts contrast (0.5)." },

{ id:"E2-2", ch:2, type:"calc-essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:3,
  q:"A company issues 8 million shares at $25.00 with a 7% gross spread and a 15% greenshoe that is fully exercised. (a) Compute the maximum greenshoe shares. (b) Compute total gross proceeds. (c) Compute the underwriting fee and the net proceeds to the issuer. (d) Explain the purpose of the greenshoe and who benefits from IPO underpricing.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> (a) Greenshoe = 15% × 8m = <b>1.2m shares</b>; total = <b>9.2m shares</b>. (b) Gross proceeds = 9.2m × $25 = <b>$230m</b>. (c) Fee = 7% × $230m = <b>$16.1m</b>; net to issuer = 230 − 16.1 = <b>$213.9m</b>.</p><p>(d) The greenshoe (over-allotment option) lets underwriters sell up to 15% more shares than the base deal, creating a short position. If the price falls below the offer price they buy back in the market, supporting the price; if it rises they exercise the option and take shares from the issuer at the offer price. It therefore stabilizes the aftermarket without exposing the bank to loss.</p><p>On <b>underpricing</b>: pricing below expected trading value transfers value from the issuer (which raises less than it could — 'money left on the table') to the allocated investors, who enjoy an immediate gain. The underwriter benefits indirectly, since it can reward its distribution clients and reduce its own placement risk — a genuine conflict of interest with the issuer it advises.</p>",
  rubric:"(a) 0.5 · (b) 0.5 · (c) 0.75 · (d) greenshoe mechanism (0.75) and underpricing beneficiaries/conflict (0.5)." },

{ id:"E2-3", ch:2, type:"essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:2,
  q:"Compare private equity and venture capital across stage, control, leverage and return profile, and explain the '2 and 20' fee structure including the role of the hurdle rate.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> <b>Venture capital</b> finances early-stage, high-growth companies, takes <b>minority</b> stakes, uses <b>no leverage</b>, and expects most investments to fail while a few large winners drive the fund's return. <b>Buyout private equity</b> acquires <b>control</b> of mature, cash-generative businesses using substantial <b>leverage</b> (LBO); returns come from debt paydown, EBITDA growth and multiple expansion, with a low but costly failure rate.</p><p><b>2 and 20:</b> the general partner charges a 2% annual management fee (on committed, later invested, capital) to fund operations, plus 20% <b>carried interest</b> on profits. The <b>hurdle rate</b> (preferred return, commonly 8%) is the threshold return limited partners must receive before the GP shares in any profit; it aligns incentives by ensuring the GP is paid for genuine outperformance rather than for merely deploying capital. A high-water mark or clawback prevents the GP from being paid twice on the same gains.</p>",
  rubric:"Four comparison dimensions handled correctly (1.0) · fee structure explained (0.5) · role/purpose of the hurdle (0.5)." },

/* ---------- Chapter 3 ---------- */
{ id:"E3-1", ch:3, type:"essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:4,
  q:"Explain the difference between the sell-side (auction) and buy-side M&A processes, describing the advisor's role in each and how a sell-side advisor creates competitive tension.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> The <b>sell-side</b> advisor runs a structured auction to maximise price and certainty for the seller: preparation and valuation; a one-page anonymous <b>teaser</b>; NDAs; the <b>CIM</b>; a first round producing non-binding indications of interest; a second round with management presentations and data-room due diligence; final binding bids with mark-ups of the purchase agreement; negotiation, signing and closing.</p><p>The <b>buy-side</b> advisor works for the acquirer: sourcing and screening targets against a strategic filter, valuing the target standalone plus synergies, coordinating due diligence, structuring the consideration and arranging financing, negotiating terms, and supporting integration planning.</p><p><b>Competitive tension</b> is created by running a genuine multi-party process: contacting several credible buyers simultaneously, imposing firm bid deadlines, keeping bidders uncertain about rivals' price levels, maintaining a credible walk-away alternative, and preserving optionality by keeping more than one bidder alive to the final round. The trade-off is between a broad auction (maximum price discovery, higher leak and disruption risk) and a targeted process (confidentiality, less tension).</p>",
  rubric:"Sell-side stages accurate (1.3) · buy-side activities accurate (1.2) · competitive tension mechanisms (1.0) · broad vs targeted trade-off (0.5)." },

{ id:"E3-2", ch:3, type:"calc-essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:4,
  q:"Acquirer A has EPS of $4.00, 100m shares and a share price of $60. Target T has net income of $150m, 50m shares and an unaffected price of $40. A offers a 25% premium, funding 60% in stock and 40% in cash borrowed at 4.5% after tax. After-tax synergies are $20m. (a) Compute the offer price and purchase equity value. (b) Compute new shares issued and after-tax interest. (c) Compute pro forma EPS and accretion/dilution. (d) Explain what would make the deal dilutive.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> (a) Offer price = $40 × 1.25 = <b>$50.00</b>; purchase equity value = $50 × 50m = <b>$2,500m</b>.</p><p>(b) Stock consideration = 60% × 2,500 = $1,500m → new shares = 1,500/60 = <b>25m</b>. Cash = 40% × 2,500 = $1,000m → after-tax interest = 4.5% × 1,000 = <b>$45m</b>.</p><p>(c) Acquirer NI = $4.00 × 100m = $400m. Pro forma NI = 400 + 150 + 20 − 45 = <b>$525m</b>. Pro forma shares = 100 + 25 = <b>125m</b>. Pro forma EPS = 525/125 = <b>$4.20</b>. Accretion = 4.20/4.00 − 1 = <b>+5.0% (accretive)</b>.</p><p>(d) The deal turns dilutive if: the premium rises so more shares must be issued or more debt raised; the stock proportion increases while the acquirer's P/E is below the P/E paid (A's P/E = 60/4 = 15×; the P/E paid = 2,500/150 = 16.7×, so the stock component alone is dilutive and is being rescued by cheap debt and synergies); the cost of debt rises above the target's earnings yield; or synergies fail to materialise. Sensitivity analysis on premium, mix and synergies is therefore standard.</p>",
  rubric:"(a) 0.7 · (b) 0.8 · (c) 1.5 with correct pro forma EPS and % · (d) at least three valid drivers of dilution incl. P/E logic (1.0)." },

{ id:"E3-3", ch:3, type:"essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:4,
  q:"Analyze why many acquisitions fail to create value for the acquirer despite a sound strategic rationale, and describe three anti-takeover defenses, evaluating whether they help or harm target shareholders.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> <b>Why deals fail.</b> (1) <b>Overpayment</b> — competitive auctions and managerial over-confidence (hubris) push the premium above the value of realisable synergies; the premium is paid on day one, the synergies are uncertain and deferred. (2) <b>Overestimated synergies</b> — especially revenue synergies, which depend on customer behaviour; cost synergies are more reliable. (3) <b>Integration failure</b> — incompatible systems, culture clash, loss of key staff and customer disruption. (4) <b>Agency problems</b> — empire-building rewarded by size-linked pay. Empirically, target shareholders capture most of the announced value while acquirers earn approximately zero or slightly negative abnormal returns.</p><p><b>Defenses.</b> <i>Poison pill</i>: crossing an ownership threshold triggers discounted share purchases by other holders, diluting the bidder — forces negotiation with the board. <i>Staggered board</i>: only part of the board is elected annually, so control requires two annual meetings — a strong deterrent. <i>White knight</i>: the target invites a friendlier bidder to outbid the hostile one.</p><p><b>Evaluation.</b> Defenses can <b>help</b> shareholders by strengthening the board's bargaining position and extracting a higher premium, and by preventing coercive or two-tier offers. They can <b>harm</b> shareholders by entrenching weak management and denying a value-enhancing premium; the empirical evidence associates strong defenses (particularly staggered boards) with lower realised premia and weaker governance. The answer therefore depends on whether the board acts as a bargaining agent or as an entrenched incumbent.</p>",
  rubric:"At least three well-argued causes of failure with empirical evidence (1.8) · three defenses correctly described (1.2) · balanced two-sided evaluation (1.0)." },

/* ---------- Chapter 4 ---------- */
{ id:"E4-1", ch:4, type:"essay", clo:"CLO2", marks:3,
  q:"Explain the two main revenue lines in asset management and why AUM growth drives franchise value. Discuss how the rise of passive investing has changed the economics.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> The two lines are (1) the <b>management fee</b>, a recurring percentage of assets under management charged regardless of performance, and (2) the <b>performance fee (carry)</b>, a share of profits above a hurdle, which is contingent and volatile.</p><p><b>AUM drives value</b> because management-fee revenue scales directly and mechanically with assets, while the cost base (portfolio managers, research, systems) rises far more slowly — so incremental AUM is highly profitable. Revenue is also recurring and predictable, which is why universal banks value asset management as a stabiliser against cyclical advisory and trading income. AUM grows through market appreciation and through net inflows; retention matters as much as performance.</p><p><b>Passive investing</b> has compressed fees industry-wide. Because most active managers underperform their benchmark after fees, capital has migrated to index funds and ETFs charging a few basis points. Consequences: falling average revenue per unit of AUM; consolidation to achieve scale; a 'barbell' outcome in which cheap passive beta and genuinely differentiated alternatives (which retain carry) both prosper while mediocre, expensive active funds are squeezed; and greater emphasis on distribution and advice as sources of value.</p>",
  rubric:"Two revenue lines correctly explained (1.0) · AUM scaling logic incl. cost base (1.0) · informed discussion of passive-driven fee compression (1.0)." },

{ id:"E4-2", ch:4, type:"calc-essay", clo:"CLO2", marks:3,
  q:"A fund with $800m AUM charges a 2% management fee and 20% carry over an 8% hurdle. In year 1 the gross return is 15%; in year 2 it is 5%. (a) Compute the investor's net return in each year. (b) Compute the manager's revenue in each year. (c) Explain the purpose of a high-water mark.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> <b>Year 1:</b> excess over hurdle = 15% − 8% = 7%; carry = 20% × 7% = 1.4%. Net return = 15% − 2% − 1.4% = <b>11.6%</b>. Manager revenue = (2% + 1.4%) × $800m = <b>$27.2m</b>.</p><p><b>Year 2:</b> gross 5% is below the 8% hurdle, so carry = <b>0</b>. Net return = 5% − 2% = <b>3.0%</b>. Manager revenue = 2% × $800m = <b>$16m</b>.</p><p>(c) A <b>high-water mark</b> records the highest NAV on which performance fees have already been paid. After a loss, no carry may be charged until the NAV exceeds that previous peak. Without it a manager could lose 20% one year, gain 20% the next and collect carry on the recovery — charging twice for the same gains and rewarding volatility rather than skill. It protects investors and aligns incentives, working alongside the hurdle rate.</p>",
  rubric:"(a) both years correct incl. zero carry below hurdle (1.2) · (b) both revenue figures (0.8) · (c) high-water mark purpose with a clear example (1.0)." },

/* ---------- Chapter 5 ---------- */
{ id:"E5-1", ch:5, type:"calc-essay", clo:"CLO2", marks:4,
  q:"A company has EBITDA of $120m, net debt of $200m, 50m diluted shares and EPS of $3.00. Peers trade at a median 9.0× EV/EBITDA and a median 16× P/E. (a) Compute the implied enterprise value, equity value and value per share using EV/EBITDA. (b) Compute the implied price using P/E. (c) Explain why the two results differ and which you would rely on. (d) Explain why the median is used rather than the mean.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> (a) Implied EV = 9.0 × $120m = <b>$1,080m</b>. Equity value = 1,080 − 200 = <b>$880m</b>. Value per share = 880/50 = <b>$17.60</b>.</p><p>(b) Implied price = 16 × $3.00 = <b>$48.00</b>.</p><p>(c) The two differ because they capitalise different quantities. EV/EBITDA values the entire business <b>before</b> financing and is capital-structure neutral; the equity value is then derived by subtracting net debt. P/E capitalises earnings <b>after</b> interest and tax, so it embeds the subject company's leverage and tax position. A large gap of this size implies the subject is far more leveraged than its peers (its net debt is deducted in method (a) but the peers' P/E reflects their own, lower, leverage), or that EPS contains non-recurring gains. I would rely on <b>EV/EBITDA</b> for cross-company comparison and treat the P/E result as a cross-check only after adjusting peers to a comparable capital structure and normalising EPS for one-off items.</p><p>(d) The <b>median</b> is used because peer sets are small and multiples are bounded below by zero but unbounded above, so distributions are right-skewed. One peer with temporarily depressed EBITDA (a very high multiple) can drag the mean far from the typical peer; the median is robust to such outliers.</p>",
  rubric:"(a) 1.2 · (b) 0.5 · (c) capital-structure explanation and reasoned choice (1.5) · (d) outlier/skew argument (0.8)." },

{ id:"E5-2", ch:5, type:"essay", clo:"CLO2", marks:4,
  q:"Analyze when capital restructuring (changing the debt/equity mix) increases firm value and what limits it. Illustrate with the credit metrics a lender would monitor.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> <b>Why leverage can add value.</b> Debt is cheaper than equity (it is senior and less risky) and interest is tax-deductible, creating a <b>tax shield</b> worth approximately t × D in a simple setting. Substituting debt for equity therefore lowers WACC = (E/V)r<sub>e</sub> + (D/V)r<sub>d</sub>(1−t) and raises firm value. Leverage can also impose discipline on management by committing free cash flow to debt service, reducing the agency cost of free cash flow.</p><p><b>What limits it.</b> As leverage rises: equity becomes riskier so beta and r<sub>e</sub> increase; lenders demand higher r<sub>d</sub> and tighter covenants; and expected <b>financial distress costs</b> grow — lost customers and suppliers, higher input prices, forced asset sales at a discount, management distraction and eventual bankruptcy costs. There is also a loss of <b>financial flexibility</b>: a highly levered firm cannot fund an attractive investment or survive a downturn. Beyond an optimum the marginal distress cost exceeds the marginal tax benefit and WACC turns upward (trade-off theory). The optimum is lower for firms with volatile cash flows, few tangible assets and high growth options.</p><p><b>Metrics monitored:</b> leverage = net debt / EBITDA (investment grade typically under ~3×; leveraged credits 4–6×+); interest coverage = EBIT / interest (comfortable above ~5×, stressed below ~2×); also fixed-charge cover, free cash flow / debt, and the debt maturity profile. Covenants are written directly against these ratios, so a restructuring must leave headroom against them.</p>",
  rubric:"Tax shield and WACC mechanics (1.2) · at least three limiting factors incl. distress costs and flexibility (1.5) · correct metrics with indicative thresholds (0.8) · recognition that the optimum varies by business risk (0.5)." },

/* ---------- Chapter 6 ---------- */
{ id:"E6-1", ch:6, type:"essay", clo:"CLO3", marks:3,
  q:"Distinguish agency brokerage, market making and proprietary trading by how each earns revenue and the risk it takes. Explain why a market maker widens its spread.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> <b>Agency brokerage:</b> the firm acts for the client, executing orders in the market and earning a <b>commission</b>. It never takes the other side, so it bears no market risk — only operational, settlement and compliance risk (best execution, client asset segregation, no front-running).</p><p><b>Market making:</b> the firm acts as <b>principal</b>, quoting two-sided prices and trading from its own inventory. It earns the <b>bid-ask spread</b> in return for supplying immediacy, and bears inventory risk, adverse-selection risk and order-processing costs.</p><p><b>Proprietary trading:</b> the firm deploys its <b>own capital</b> to profit from price movements, earning <b>trading gains</b> and bearing full directional market risk. The distinction from market making is intent — facilitating client flow versus expressing a view; the Volcker Rule restricts most prop trading precisely because that line is hard to police.</p><p><b>Why spreads widen:</b> when volatility rises (inventory can move against the dealer faster), when volume/depth is thin (harder to offload a position), when the dealer suspects <b>informed</b> trading (adverse selection), when its inventory is already unbalanced, and when funding or capital costs rise. The spread is therefore a direct, observable measure of market liquidity.</p>",
  rubric:"Three activities correctly distinguished by revenue and risk (1.8) · at least three valid reasons for spread widening (0.9) · spread as liquidity measure (0.3)." },

{ id:"E6-2", ch:6, type:"calc-essay", clo:"CLO3", marks:3,
  q:"An investor buys 2,000 shares at $60 with a 50% initial margin and a 30% maintenance margin. (a) Compute the equity posted and the loan. (b) Compute the margin-call price. (c) If the price falls to $40, compute the account equity and state whether a call is triggered. (d) Compute the investor's return on equity at $40 and compare with the unlevered return.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> (a) Position value = 2,000 × $60 = $120,000. Equity posted = 50% × 120,000 = <b>$60,000</b>; loan = <b>$60,000</b>.</p><p>(b) Call price = P × (1 − initial)/(1 − maintenance) = 60 × 0.50/0.70 = <b>$42.86</b>.</p><p>(c) At $40: market value = 2,000 × 40 = $80,000; equity = 80,000 − 60,000 = <b>$20,000</b>, which is 20,000/80,000 = <b>25%</b> of market value — below the 30% maintenance requirement, and $40 is below the $42.86 call price, so a <b>margin call is triggered</b>. The investor must deposit cash or securities to restore 30% equity, or the broker will liquidate the position.</p><p>(d) Levered return = (20,000 − 60,000)/60,000 = <b>−66.7%</b> (ignoring interest). Unlevered return = (40 − 60)/60 = <b>−33.3%</b>. Leverage of 2× has exactly doubled the loss — the symmetric counterpart of doubling a gain, and the reason margin lending is a risk-managed product.</p>",
  rubric:"(a) 0.5 · (b) 0.7 · (c) equity, ratio and correct call conclusion (1.0) · (d) both returns with leverage interpretation (0.8)." },

{ id:"E6-3", ch:6, type:"essay", clo:"CLO3", marks:3, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain how securities lending enables short selling, identify the costs and risks to the short seller, and discuss why regulators monitor short selling closely.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> <b>Mechanism.</b> A lender (typically a fund or custodian holding a long-term position) lends shares against collateral and a borrow fee. The short seller (1) borrows the shares, (2) sells them in the market at today's price, (3) later buys them back, and (4) returns them to the lender. Profit = sale price − repurchase price − borrow fee − any dividends that must be passed through to the lender.</p><p><b>Costs and risks.</b> The borrow fee (which can spike for hard-to-borrow stocks); dividend and corporate-action pass-throughs; margin/collateral requirements on the position; <b>unlimited theoretical loss</b>, since the price can rise without bound while a long position can only fall to zero; <b>recall risk</b>, if the lender demands the shares back, forcing the short to cover at a bad moment; and <b>short squeeze</b> risk, where rising prices force crowded shorts to buy simultaneously, driving prices higher still.</p><p><b>Why regulators care.</b> Short selling aids price discovery, corrects overvaluation and adds liquidity, and shorts have historically exposed frauds. But it can amplify falls in a crisis, and naked shorting (selling without arranging a borrow) risks settlement failure. Regulators therefore impose locate/borrow requirements, disclosure of large short positions, and sometimes temporary bans. Vietnam restricts covered short selling and regulates margin ratios directly as a macro-prudential tool.</p>",
  rubric:"Four-step mechanism with correct profit formula (1.2) · at least three costs/risks incl. unlimited loss and recall/squeeze (1.0) · balanced regulatory discussion (0.8)." },

/* ================= ADDITIONAL BANK — mid-term & final coverage ================= */

/* ---------- Chapter 1 ---------- */
{ id:"E1-4", ch:1, type:"essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:4, exam:"midterm",
  q:"Compare an investment bank with a commercial bank across the six criteria used in the course: business model, sources of income, method of raising funds, asset–liability management orientation, financial leverage and legal framework.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> <b>(1) Business model.</b> The IB channels capital from surplus to deficit units through the capital market; capital reaches the issuer and the <b>investment risk stays with the investor</b>. The CB interposes its balance sheet — takes deposits, makes loans, and absorbs the <b>credit risk</b> itself.</p><p><b>(2) Income.</b> IB: fees (underwriting, brokerage, trusteeship and asset management, advisory, transactions), interest spread in wholesale banking, and proprietary trading P&amp;L. CB: net interest margin plus payment and service fees.</p><p><b>(3) Raising funds.</b> The IB's key disadvantage is that it <b>may not take public deposits</b>. Its largest source is <b>secured borrowing</b> — repo, sell-and-buy-back, securities lending, short selling, margin agreements — collateralised by liquid securities; the second is unsecured borrowing (bank loans, bonds, notes, commercial paper); then equity. The CB funds itself principally with customer deposits.</p><p><b>(4) ALM orientation.</b> The CB raises funds first and then decides deployment — <i>funding determines use of funds</i>, an <b>asset-management</b> orientation. The IB raises funding only against actual investment needs — <i>use of funds determines funding</i>, a <b>liability-management</b> orientation.</p><p><b>(5) Leverage.</b> The CB is low-leverage: many assets are loans and long-held investments, and leverage is constrained by Basel capital-adequacy ratios. The IB is high-leverage because most assets are liquid and readily pledged. Accepting and controlling high leverage is the core art of managing an IB.</p><p><b>(6) Legal framework.</b> Supervisor varies: the US SEC (and, after 2008, also the Federal Reserve); the central bank in Europe; the <b>State Securities Commission</b> in Vietnam. Add the summary differences: IB operates mainly in the capital market with low profit stability and high operating risk; the CB in the money market with high stability and lower risk.</p>",
  rubric:"All six criteria addressed (3.0 — 0.5 each) · correct direction of each contrast (0.6) · Vietnamese supervisory context (0.4)." },

{ id:"E1-5", ch:1, type:"case", clo:"CLO1", marks:4, exam:"final",
  q:"A Vietnamese securities company operates brokerage, proprietary trading, underwriting and research under one roof. Identify the conflicts of interest that arise between these divisions and design the control framework you would recommend.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer — conflicts.</b> (i) <b>Research vs. investment banking</b>: pressure to publish favourable reports to win or protect an underwriting or advisory mandate. (ii) <b>Research vs. proprietary trading</b>: pressure to publish reports that benefit the firm's own book — for example a 'buy' recommendation while the desk sells. (iii) <b>Proprietary trading vs. brokerage</b>: the temptation to front-run client orders, or to allocate favourable fills to the firm. (iv) <b>Investment banking vs. asset management</b>: placing an underwritten issue into managed client portfolios to clear inventory. (v) <b>Underwriting vs. investors</b>: allocation of hot IPO shares to favoured accounts.</p><p><b>Control framework.</b> (1) An <b>information barrier</b> separating the <b>private side</b> (investment-banking staff holding material non-public information) from the <b>public side</b> (research, sales and trading), enforced physically and in system access, with documented <b>wall-crossing</b> procedures and a restricted/watch list maintained by a control room. (2) <b>Client order priority</b> — client orders execute ahead of the firm's; separate accounts, order books and personnel for proprietary trading. (3) <b>Research independence</b> — research reporting lines and compensation separated from banking revenue; no pre-publication review by bankers; mandatory disclosure of banking relationships in reports. (4) <b>Allocation policy</b> — a written, objective IPO allocation policy with records. (5) <b>Personal account dealing</b> rules and pre-clearance. (6) <b>Compliance monitoring</b> — surveillance of trading around research publication and around mandates, plus internal audit. (7) <b>Governance</b> — an independent compliance function reporting to the board, and staff training with disciplinary consequences.</p>",
  rubric:"At least four genuine conflicts correctly identified (1.6) · information barrier and private/public side explained (1.0) · at least four concrete controls (1.0) · governance/enforcement point (0.4)." },

/* ---------- Chapter 2 ---------- */
{ id:"E2-4", ch:2, type:"essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:4, exam:"midterm",
  q:"A Vietnamese joint-stock company intends to make an initial public offering. Set out the conditions it must satisfy under the Law on Securities 2019, classify the issuance methods available to it, and describe the issuance process.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer — conditions.</b> Paid-up <b>charter capital of at least VND 30 billion</b> by book value at registration; <b>profitable operations in the two preceding years</b> with no accumulated losses to the year of registration; an offering and capital-use plan approved by the General Meeting of Shareholders; at least <b>15% of voting shares sold to at least 100 non-major shareholders</b> (with an adjusted proportion for larger charter capital); a commitment by major shareholders to hold at least 20% of charter capital for at least one year; the issuer is not under criminal prosecution; an <b>securities company advising on the registration dossier</b> (unless the issuer is itself a securities company) — the 2019 Law requires an adviser, not an underwriter; and a commitment to list or register for trading after the offering. A depository account and a blocked escrow account for proceeds are also required.</p><p><b>Classification of methods.</b> <i>By offering round</i>: initial (IPO) vs. secondary/seasoned. <i>By target investor</i>: public offering, private placement, rights issue to existing shareholders, ESOP. <i>By pricing method</i>: fixed price, bookbuilding, auction. <i>By distribution method</i>: direct issuance vs. issuance through an underwriting intermediary.</p><p><b>Process.</b> Preparation and appointment of the underwriter → due diligence (legal, financial, business) → registration dossier and prospectus filed with the SSC → valuation and price range → roadshow and bookbuilding → pricing, distribution and allocation → reporting of results, share registration and listing → aftermarket stabilisation using the greenshoe (up to 15% of the base deal).</p>",
  rubric:"At least five conditions with correct thresholds (1.5) · all four classification axes (1.3) · process in correct sequence (1.2)." },

/* ---------- Chapter 3 ---------- */
{ id:"E3-4", ch:3, type:"case", clo:"CLO1", marks:4, exam:"midterm",
  q:"You advise the board of a listed Vietnamese company that has received an unsolicited, below-value takeover approach. Explain the defensive tactics available, distinguishing preventive from reactive measures, and advise the board on the fiduciary considerations.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer — preventive defences.</b> <i>Poison pill</i> (rights plan) diluting a bidder that crosses a threshold; <i>staggered board</i> so control requires two annual meetings; <i>supermajority provisions</i> in the charter; <i>golden parachutes</i> raising the cost of a change of control; <i>cross-shareholdings</i> with friendly parties.</p><p><b>Reactive / counter-attack tactics.</b> <i>White knight</i> — invite a friendlier bidder to outbid; <i>crown jewel</i> — sell or option the asset the bidder most wants; <i>Pac-Man</i> — counter-bid for the raider; <i>greenmail</i> — repurchase the raider's stake at a premium; <i>litigation and regulatory challenge</i> to gain time; <i>recapitalisation</i> with a special dividend to make the target less attractive.</p><p><b>Fiduciary considerations.</b> The board's duty runs to the <b>company and its shareholders</b>, not to preserving management's positions. It should: (i) obtain an independent <b>valuation and fairness opinion</b> to establish whether the offer genuinely undervalues the company; (ii) document that any defence is a proportionate response to a real threat, not entrenchment; (iii) manage conflicts — directors and executives with parachutes or continuing roles should be recused from key decisions; (iv) consider whether an auction would obtain better value for shareholders than resistance; (v) comply with disclosure obligations and equal treatment of shareholders. Empirically, strong defences (especially staggered boards) are associated with <b>lower realised premia</b>, so blanket resistance may itself destroy shareholder value — the defensible position is to use defences as <b>bargaining leverage</b> to raise the price, not to defeat every bid.</p>",
  rubric:"At least four preventive and three reactive tactics correctly described (1.8) · clear preventive/reactive distinction (0.5) · fiduciary analysis incl. entrenchment risk and fairness opinion (1.2) · balanced conclusion citing evidence (0.5)." },

/* ---------- Chapter 4 ---------- */
{ id:"E4-3", ch:4, type:"essay", clo:"CLO2", marks:4, exam:"final",
  q:"Describe the operations of investment fund management: raising capital for the fund, investing the capital, and the supporting arrangements. Distinguish open-end funds, closed-end funds and ETFs.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer — raising capital.</b> Design the fund (objective, strategy, benchmark, fee schedule, term); obtain regulatory approval and appoint the <b>supervisory bank / custodian</b>; prepare the prospectus and fund charter; distribute units through the manager's channels, securities companies, banks or advisors. Public funds must meet a minimum capital raised and a minimum number of investors to be established.</p><p><b>Investing the capital.</b> Apply the mandate and benchmark → strategic asset allocation and tactical tilts → security selection driven by research → execution with best execution and cost control → risk control through diversification and regulatory limits (single-issuer caps, restrictions on borrowing and lending) → monitoring and rebalancing.</p><p><b>Supporting arrangements.</b> Independent custody and supervision of assets; daily valuation and <b>NAV per unit = (total assets − total liabilities) ÷ units outstanding</b>; processing of subscriptions and redemptions; reporting to investors and the regulator with audited annual accounts; and fee collection (management fee, and performance fee where permitted, subject to a hurdle and high-water mark).</p><p><b>Fund types.</b> An <b>open-end fund</b> continuously issues and redeems units at NAV, so its size fluctuates with flows and it must hold liquidity for redemptions. A <b>closed-end fund</b> has a fixed number of units traded on the secondary market with <b>no redemption obligation</b>, so it can hold illiquid assets but its price may deviate from NAV (trading at a discount or premium). An <b>ETF</b> is an open-end structure whose units trade intraday on an exchange, with the market price kept close to NAV by <b>authorised-participant arbitrage</b> through in-kind creation and redemption.</p>",
  rubric:"Raising capital incl. custodian and prospectus (1.0) · investment process (1.0) · NAV formula and supporting arrangements (1.0) · three fund types correctly distinguished (1.0)." },

{ id:"E4-4", ch:4, type:"calc-essay", clo:"CLO2", marks:3, exam:"final",
  q:"A fund has AUM of VND 1,000 billion, charges a 1.75% management fee and 20% carry over a 7% hurdle, and delivers a gross return of 12%. (a) Compute the performance fee, the investor's net return and the manager's total revenue. (b) Explain how the answer changes if the gross return is 5%. (c) Explain the purpose of a high-water mark.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> (a) Excess over hurdle = 12% − 7% = 5%; performance fee = 20% × 5% = <b>1.0%</b>. Investor net return = 12% − 1.75% − 1.0% = <b>9.25%</b>. Manager revenue = (1.75% + 1.0%) × 1,000 = <b>VND 27.5 billion</b>.</p><p>(b) At a 5% gross return the hurdle of 7% is <b>not cleared</b>, so no carry is earned. Investor net return = 5% − 1.75% = <b>3.25%</b>, and manager revenue falls to 1.75% × 1,000 = <b>VND 17.5 billion</b>. Note that the management fee is charged regardless of performance — which is precisely why gathering and retaining AUM, not just generating returns, drives an asset manager's profitability.</p><p>(c) A <b>high-water mark</b> records the highest NAV on which performance fees have already been paid; no further carry may be charged until the NAV exceeds that previous peak. Without it, a manager who loses 20% in one year and gains 20% the next would collect carry on the recovery — charging twice for the same gains and rewarding volatility rather than skill. Together with the hurdle it aligns the manager's incentives with the investor's.</p>",
  rubric:"(a) three figures correct (1.2) · (b) zero carry recognised with both figures and the AUM insight (1.0) · (c) high-water mark purpose with a numerical illustration (0.8)." },

/* ---------- Chapter 5 ---------- */
{ id:"E5-3", ch:5, type:"essay", clo:"CLO2", marks:4, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain the equitization of a state-owned enterprise: its purpose, the steps in the process, and the principal valuation difficulty. Why is this step so heavily scrutinised?",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer — purpose.</b> Equitization (cổ phần hóa) converts an SOE into a joint-stock company and sells part of the state's holding, in order to mobilise capital from outside investors, improve governance and operating efficiency through private ownership and outside directors, reduce the state's fiscal burden, and deepen the securities market. Legally it is also the only route by which the enterprise can later issue shares publicly and list, since only a joint-stock company may do so.</p><p><b>Process.</b> (1) Establish the steering committee and assisting team. (2) Inventory and classify assets; settle financial matters and outstanding obligations. (3) <b>Value the enterprise</b>. (4) Prepare and obtain approval of the equitization plan — charter capital, and allocation of shares among the state, strategic investors, employees/trade union and the public. (5) Sell shares by IPO through the exchange, by sale to strategic investors, and by preferential sale to employees. (6) Register the joint-stock company, hold the first General Meeting of Shareholders and hand over. (7) Register for trading or listing.</p><p><b>The valuation difficulty.</b> The contested elements are <b>land-use rights</b>, brand value and goodwill. Land-use rights are frequently the largest single item of value and the hardest to price: their value depends on location, permitted use and the duration and form of the allocation, and there may be no comparable market transaction.</p><p><b>Why it is scrutinised.</b> Because the valuation determines how much public wealth transfers to private buyers. Undervaluation transfers state assets cheaply to insiders or strategic investors — a recurring source of loss and of prosecution; overvaluation makes the offering fail and wastes the process. The advisor's methodology must therefore be transparent, defensible and independently reviewable.</p>",
  rubric:"Purpose incl. legal precondition for listing (1.0) · at least five process steps in order (1.4) · land-use-rights valuation problem identified (1.0) · public-interest rationale for scrutiny (0.6)." },

{ id:"E5-4", ch:5, type:"essay", clo:"CLO2", marks:4, exam:"final",
  q:"A profitable, family-controlled company plans to list. Explain the listing conditions and process, evaluate the advantages and disadvantages of listing for this owner, and explain what governance restructuring you would recommend beforehand.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer — conditions and process.</b> Conditions typically cover charter capital (≥ VND 30 billion), operating history as a joint-stock company, profitability (ROE ≥ 5%, profitable years, no accumulated losses), ownership dispersion (≥ 15% of voting shares held by ≥ 100 non-major shareholders), absence of long-overdue debts, and lock-up undertakings from insiders. The advisory process runs: eligibility assessment and gap analysis → restructuring and preparation → valuation and choice of exchange and timing → dossier and prospectus → submission and approval → first trading day → ongoing disclosure and investor relations.</p><p><b>Advantages for this owner:</b> access to capital on better terms and a public market price for the shares; liquidity and partial monetisation for the family; reputation and credibility with banks, customers and partners; shares usable as acquisition currency and for employee incentives; a framework for succession.</p><p><b>Disadvantages:</b> the cost of listing and continuing compliance; extensive continuous disclosure, including of matters a family firm may prefer private; pressure for short-term results and exposure to market sentiment; dilution and loss of absolute control, with exposure to takeover; and personal liability and scrutiny for directors.</p><p><b>Governance restructuring recommended beforehand:</b> appoint genuinely <b>independent directors</b> and separate the roles of chair and chief executive; establish <b>audit, remuneration and nomination committees</b> with real authority; appoint a reputable external auditor and build an internal audit function reporting to the audit committee; clean up and formalise <b>related-party transactions</b>, which in a family firm are the single greatest investor concern; strengthen disclosure capability, including bilingual reporting; and introduce minority-protection provisions in the charter. The commercial argument is direct: closing the <b>governance discount</b> raises the multiple achieved at IPO, so this work pays for itself.</p>",
  rubric:"Conditions with figures (1.0) · process (0.8) · balanced advantages and disadvantages specific to a family owner (1.2) · concrete governance recommendations linked to valuation (1.0)." },

/* ---------- Chapter 6 ---------- */
{ id:"E6-4", ch:6, type:"essay", clo:"CLO3", marks:4, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain the brokerage process from account opening to settlement, describe the main order types used on Vietnamese exchanges, and explain the matching priority rules.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer — process.</b> (1) <b>Open the account</b> — client identification, brokerage contract, risk profiling and registration with the depository. (2) <b>Deposit</b> funds and securities into the designated accounts. (3) <b>Receive the order</b> — in person, by telephone or online — and verify available purchasing power or securities balance. (4) <b>Enter the order</b> into the exchange's trading system. (5) <b>Matching</b> by the exchange and confirmation to the client. (6) <b>Clearing and settlement</b> through the depository on the T+n cycle. (7) <b>Reporting</b> and account statements. Throughout, the broker owes duties of best execution, segregation of client assets and client order priority.</p><p><b>Order types.</b> <b>LO</b> (limit order) executes at the stated price or better — certainty of price but not of execution. <b>MP / MTL</b> (market order) executes at the best available price — certainty of execution but not of price; any unexecuted remainder is typically converted to a limit order or cancelled. <b>ATO</b> (at-the-open) participates only in the opening call auction and has priority over LO orders in that matching. <b>ATC</b> (at-the-close) participates only in the closing call auction. Prices must lie within the daily band between the <b>floor</b> and the <b>ceiling</b> around the reference price.</p><p><b>Matching priority.</b> <b>Price priority first</b> — the highest bid and the lowest ask are matched first. <b>Then time priority</b> — among orders at the same price, the one entered earlier is matched first. In call auctions the system selects the price that maximises executed volume, with ATO/ATC orders taking precedence over limit orders at the same price.</p>",
  rubric:"Seven-step process in order (1.4) · four order types with correct behaviour (1.4) · price-then-time priority stated correctly (0.8) · duties/price band mentioned (0.4)." },

{ id:"E6-5", ch:6, type:"case", clo:"CLO3", marks:4, exam:"final",
  q:"A securities company wishes to expand its margin lending book significantly. Explain how margin lending works, quantify the risk with an example, and advise the board on the risk-management framework and the systemic concerns a regulator would raise.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer — mechanics.</b> Margin lending is a loan from the securities company to a client to buy securities, secured by the securities in the client's account. The <b>initial margin</b> is the client's required equity contribution; the <b>maintenance margin</b> is the minimum equity ratio thereafter. Buying power = Equity ÷ initial margin%; equity = market value − loan; and the <b>margin call price = purchase price × (1 − initial margin) ÷ (1 − maintenance margin)</b>. Positions are marked to market daily; if equity falls below the maintenance level the firm issues a <b>margin call</b>, and if it is unmet the firm <b>force-sells</b>.</p><p><b>Quantified example.</b> A client buys at VND 50,000 with 50% initial and 30% maintenance margin. Call price = 50,000 × 0.50 ÷ 0.70 = <b>VND 35,714</b>. Leverage is 2×, so a −20% price move produces roughly a <b>−40%</b> return on the client's equity; a −29% move wipes out more than half of it and triggers the call.</p><p><b>Risk-management framework.</b> Lend only against an <b>eligible securities list</b> screened for liquidity, free float and volatility; impose <b>concentration limits</b> per security, per client and per group of related clients; set loan-to-value ratios below the regulatory maximum with buffers for volatile names; mark to market intra-day with automated alerts; define and enforce a documented margin-call and force-sale procedure with clear timelines; stress-test the book against a market-wide fall (e.g. −20% and −30%); monitor the firm's own funding liquidity, since the book must be financed; and ensure legal enforceability of the collateral.</p><p><b>Systemic concerns.</b> Margin lending is <b>pro-cyclical</b>: falling prices trigger calls, forced selling pushes prices lower and triggers further calls — a downward spiral that can turn a correction into a crash. Concentration in a few popular stocks means many clients are forced to sell the same names simultaneously. Regulators therefore cap margin ratios directly, restrict eligible securities, and treat the aggregate margin balance as a macro-prudential indicator. The board should recognise that the revenue is attractive precisely because the risk is real, and size the book accordingly.</p>",
  rubric:"Mechanics with correct formulae (1.0) · worked numerical example incl. call price and levered return (1.0) · at least four concrete controls (1.2) · pro-cyclicality and macro-prudential argument (0.8)." }

];
